GLOSSARY = [
    ("Action threshold", "An action threshold is a value or a finding that you agree before the scouting. The threshold changes the task from monitoring to a response. It can be a number of organisms, a trend in some readings, a severity value, or one confirmed finding when the tolerance is zero."),
    ("Alate", "An aphid with wings. A colony makes aphids with wings when it has too many aphids. These aphids can go away and start a new colony."),
    ("Antagonist", "A microbe that is a beneficial organism and that controls a plant pathogen. It uses the same space and nutrients as the pathogen, it makes substances that stop the pathogen, or it controls the pathogen by other methods."),
    ("Asexual", "The production of offspring without sex. Indoors, most groups of pests and pathogens increase in number by this method."),
    ("Axil", "The area where a leaf or branch connects to the stem. This area has protection, and thus it is important to examine it."),
    ("Biosecurity", "The controls that stop the entry of pests and pathogens into the facility, and that stop their movement in the facility."),
    ("Bronzing", "A change of color to bronze-brown. Injury from pests that eat the plant, or stress, causes it. The term shows the color that you see. It does not give the name of the cause."),
    ("CAPA", "Corrective and preventive action. Put the event in containment, find the cause, and correct it. Change the condition that let the event occur. Then make sure that the change had an effect."),
    ("Chlorosis", "The green color of the plant tissue becomes less."),
    ("Conidium / conidia", "A spore of a fungus that the fungus makes without sex. It is for the movement of the fungus to other plants. One spore is a conidium. More than one spore are conidia."),
    ("Cornicle", "One of the two short tubes near the rear of an aphid. The cornicles are the sign that most quickly confirms that an insect is an aphid."),
    ("Crown rot", "Disease at the crown, where the stem connects to the root system."),
    ("Cupping / tacoing", "Leaf edges that bend up or down. Heat, humidity, chemical injury and mite damage cause it. Thus examine the plant for the organism before you make a decision on the cause."),
    ("Damping-off", "A seedling or cutting collapses suddenly because of root pathogens or crown pathogens. Pythium or Fusarium frequently cause it."),
    ("Dew-point margin", "The dew-point margin is the difference between the temperature of the leaf or surface and the dew point of the air around it. When the temperature of the surface is less than the dew point, condensation occurs. If the dew-point margin is small, condensation is near."),
    ("Dissolved oxygen", "The oxygen in the irrigation solution. Roots must have it. When the quantity is low, the roots have stress, and the conditions help some root diseases."),
    ("Dudding", "A term of production for weak cannabis plants with slow growth and flowers with weak development. The term shows how the plants look. It does not give a diagnosis, and it is not a different name for HLVd."),
    ("Entomopathogenic", "An organism that can infect and kill insects or mites. The term is for the fungi, bacteria and nematodes that you use as biological controls."),
    ("Exclusion", "All the controls that stop the entry of a pest, a pathogen or material that has no approval into the controlled crop system."),
    ("FRAC / IRAC", "When you use one mode of action again and again, the pests or pathogens that stay are the most resistant to the product. These organisms increase in number. The name of this effect is resistance. FRAC and IRAC are the systems that put fungicides and insecticides in groups by mode of action. Thus a program can change from one group to a different group, and does not apply the same pressure again and again."),
    ("Honeydew", "A tacky liquid with sugar. Aphids, whiteflies and other insects that eat sap make it as waste."),
    ("Incidence", "Incidence is the proportion of plants or sites that have the problem. You record incidence and severity as two different values."),
    ("Inoculum", "Pathogen material that can start a new infection, for example spores, debris with the infection, or water with contamination."),
    ("Instar", "The stage of an arthropod that is not an adult, between one molt and the next molt."),
    ("Larva", "A stage that is not an adult and that eats. It does not look the same as the adult. In fungus gnats, thrips and caterpillars, the larva causes the damage and it is important to identify it."),
    ("LOQ", "Limit of quantification. The lowest concentration for which a laboratory method gives a result with satisfactory performance. At a concentration less than the LOQ, the method cannot give a number."),
    ("Meristem", "The tissue at a shoot tip that divides and makes all the new growth above it."),
    ("Mycelium", "The group of thin tubes that is the body of a fungus."),
    ("Mold", "Growth of a fungus that you can see. The standard spelling in New Zealand is different from the spelling in this paper. Imported references use the spelling of this paper. The two spellings are for the same growth."),
    ("Necrosis", "Plant tissue that died."),
    ("Nymph", "A stage of an arthropod that looks the same as the adult, but is not an adult."),
    ("Oomycete", "An organism with some of the properties of a fungus, from a group that is different from the fungi. Pythium is an oomycete. It is not a fungus."),
    ("Oospore", "A spore with thick walls for survival, which some oomycetes make. It can stay in a system between crops."),
    ("Parasite", "An organism that is in or on a host and gets nutrients from the host. Usually the parasite does not kill the host. A parasitoid kills the one host that it uses."),
    ("Parasitoid", "An organism, in IPM usually a small wasp. The stage of the parasitoid that is not an adult is in or on one host and kills the host."),
    ("Parthenogenesis", "Parthenogenesis is the production of offspring without fertilization. Thus one female can start a colony. The number of pests increases without the time that is necessary to find a male. The number of some indoor pests increases by this method."),
    ("Pathogen", "An organism or agent that causes disease."),
    ("Petiole", "The stalk that connects a leaf blade to the stem. It is a good area to examine for mites and for the first symptoms."),
    ("Preventive / curative", "A preventive product is a product that you apply before a problem starts, to keep the pressure low. A curative product is a product that you apply after you confirm the problem, to decrease the problem."),
    ("Predator", "A beneficial organism that eats many prey organisms during its life cycle."),
    ("Pupa", "The stage between larva and adult in insects with complete metamorphosis. It does not usually eat. It is frequently in the media or in debris, where a spray does not touch it."),
    ("Pycnidium", "A small fruiting body of a fungus, with the shape of a flask, that makes conidia. In a lesion of Septoria it is a dark point in the middle of the lesion."),
    ("REI", "Restricted entry interval. The period after an application. In this period, no person can go into the area where you applied the product. A person can go into the area only if the person uses the specified PPE and obeys the specified conditions."),
    ("Release", "The quality decision, with a record, that lets you move or supply a plant, material or batch that you hold. You make this decision when the necessary evidence and approvals are complete."),
    ("Residue limit", "The maximum limit or action limit of a residue for the release of a batch. The residue must be less than the limit. A limit for a substance is not approval to apply the substance."),
    ("Rogue / roguing", "To remove a plant with a disease or pests from the production system and to destroy it."),
    ("Russeting", "Rough tissue with a bronze-brown color that injury from pests that eat the plant, or other stress, causes."),
    ("Severity", "How much of one plant or one site has the problem. You record severity and incidence as two different values. Incidence is the number of plants or sites that have the problem."),
    ("Sachet", "A small bag that releases beneficial organisms into the crop gradually, if the necessary conditions are correct."),
    ("Sanitation", "The task to clean and, when it is necessary, to disinfect a specified surface or system with a validated method. The method removes debris and decreases the quantity of live contamination."),
    ("Scouting", "An inspection of the plants on a route on the map, which you do again and again. You use it to find, identify and count pests and diseases, and to monitor the trend of the condition of the crop."),
    ("Sooty mold", "A dark fungus on honeydew. It shows that insects that eat sap are above it. It is not usually the primary pathogen of the plant."),
    ("Sprench", "An application with a volume between a spray and a drench. You apply it to the bottom of the plant and to the surface of the media."),
    ("Spore", "A fungus, or an organism with some of the properties of a fungus, makes spores. A spore lets the organism increase in number or survive. The type of spore changes how you collect samples and how you control the spores. Debris between crops is important, because spores can stay in it."),
    ("Stippling", "The many very small pale points that stay where mites, or pests of the same type, remove the content of cells one by one."),
    ("Systemic resistance", "A condition in all the plant that a stimulus starts. It can make the plant more resistant to a pathogen. It is not evidence of control of a pathogen that is in the plant."),
    ("Vector", "Each item or organism that moves a pest or pathogen from one area to a different area. A vector can be an insect, a tool, or the hands of a person."),
    ("Viroid", "A small RNA molecule that can infect a plant, with no protein shell. A viroid has a smaller number of parts than a virus. HLVd is a viroid."),
    ("Zoospore", "A spore that moves in water. Oomycetes, for example Pythium, make zoospores. Thus the disease moves through a water system."),
    ("ACVM", "The New Zealand regulations for agricultural compounds and veterinary medicines. MPI controls the regulations."),
    ("EPA", "The Environmental Protection Authority of New Zealand. It sets the approvals and controls for dangerous substances and new organisms."),
    ("HSNO", "The regulations of the Hazardous Substances and New Organisms Act. The EPA controls the regulations."),
    ("Inhalation / non-inhalation line", "The decision if a lot can become a product that a patient inhales. The decision changes which regulations for the active ingredients of pesticides apply to the lot, and it changes the consequences for quality. You must make the decision before you select an input."),
    ("MPI", "The Ministry for Primary Industries of New Zealand. It controls ACVM and the biosecurity pathways for inputs and for imported organisms."),
    ("Minimum quality standard", "The quality requirements in the regulations that medicinal cannabis materials and products must obey before you supply them in New Zealand."),
    ("Approved-input register", "The controlled list of the products and organisms that the site accepts. For each item, the site examines the regulations, the safety, the test methods, the compatibility and the operations before it accepts the item."),
    ("Batch hold", "A quality status that stops the movement or release of a batch while the evidence or an investigation is not complete."),
    ("Trade waste", "Liquid waste from commercial sites or industrial sites that goes into the wastewater network, with the controls of Watercare and of Auckland."),
]
